Chapter: CatStillsSpec
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter CatStills
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 CatStills: seven baked pose tables and the index that selects them.

 HALF A MEGABYTE OF GENERATED LITERALS, WHICH IS THE POINT. This chapter is the
 largest single file in the port -- 2,198 StillPt records in one list literal for
 the rest pose alone -- and nothing about it can be wrong arithmetically. What
 can go wrong is that an arm's front end will not swallow a list literal that
 long, or will swallow it slowly enough to matter. A generated table nothing
 cites is a table nothing compiles, and it is the thing in this port most likely
 to find a limit.

 THE VALUES ARE CAPTURED AND SAY SO. bake_stills.py generates this chapter from
 the art; there is no definition to derive a want from, and the oracle for
 whether the art is right is the blitter diff against the golden PNG. So the
 sizes below are counted from the tables and the file's job is to notice a table
 that changed size, emptied, or stopped compiling -- not to re-judge the drawing.

 THE DISPATCH IS THE ONE PIECE OF ACTUAL CODE. `cat-polys-for` is a seven-way
 chain of index tests with a fallback to the empty list, so every arm is graded:
 an off-by-one in the middle of that chain returns a real pose for the wrong
 index, which draws a cat in the wrong shape rather than failing. The fallback is
 graded at both ends -- -1 and 7 -- because a chain like this is written with `if
 i == 0` and a missing guard fails at exactly one end.

 THE POSES ARE NOT ALL DISTINCT IN SIZE, and that is why the point counts are
 here beside the polygon counts. Four of the seven have 34 polygons and 1,715
 points, so a dispatch that confused coil with collapse would pass a
 polygon-count check; the first colour and the counts together are what separate
 them, and even then two identical-sized poses differ only in coordinates. This
 file does not claim to tell those apart -- the whole-frame check does.

 We say:

Section: Counting

  pts-in : List StillPoly, Integer -> Integer
  pts-in (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0
    else let p = list-at ps i in list-length p.pts + pts-in ps (i + 1)

  grads-in : List StillPoly, Integer -> Integer
  grads-in (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0
    else let p = list-at ps i in list-length p.grad + grads-in ps (i + 1)

Section: The Seven Tables

 Polygon counts, in pose order: rest, stride, frozen, coil, flight, land,
 collapse.

  poly-got : List Integer
  poly-got = [list-length pose-rest-polys, list-length pose-stride-polys, list-length pose-frozen-polys, list-length pose-coil-polys, list-length pose-flight-polys, list-length pose-land-polys, list-length pose-collapse-polys]

  poly-want : List Integer
  poly-want = [46, 46, 47, 34, 34, 34, 34]

  pts-got : List Integer
  pts-got = [pts-in pose-rest-polys 0, pts-in pose-stride-polys 0, pts-in pose-frozen-polys 0, pts-in pose-coil-polys 0, pts-in pose-flight-polys 0, pts-in pose-land-polys 0, pts-in pose-collapse-polys 0]

  pts-want : List Integer
  pts-want = [2198, 2198, 2197, 1715, 1715, 1715, 1715]

 NO POSE CARRIES A GRADIENT. Every cat polygon is solid; the gradients in this
 port are the bull's. That is an assertion and not an observation -- if a
 regenerated table started carrying them, the renderer would take a path this
 chapter has never taken.

  grad-got : List Integer
  grad-got = [grads-in pose-rest-polys 0, grads-in pose-stride-polys 0, grads-in pose-frozen-polys 0, grads-in pose-coil-polys 0, grads-in pose-flight-polys 0, grads-in pose-land-polys 0, grads-in pose-collapse-polys 0]

  grad-want : List Integer
  grad-want = [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]

Section: The Dispatch

 Seven valid indices and both ends of the fallback.

  disp-got : List Integer
  disp-got = [list-length (cat-polys-for 0), list-length (cat-polys-for 1), list-length (cat-polys-for 2), list-length (cat-polys-for 3), list-length (cat-polys-for 4), list-length (cat-polys-for 5), list-length (cat-polys-for 6), list-length (cat-polys-for 7), list-length (cat-polys-for (0 - 1))]

  disp-want : List Integer
  disp-want = [46, 46, 47, 34, 34, 34, 34, 0, 0]

Section: Spot Values

 The first polygon's colour is the same slate in every pose, and the first point
 of each pose differs -- which is what says the seven tables are seven tables and
 not one table selected seven times.

  spot-got : List Real
  spot-got = [(list-at (list-at pose-rest-polys 0).pts 0).x, (list-at (list-at pose-stride-polys 0).pts 0).x, (list-at (list-at pose-frozen-polys 0).pts 0).x, (list-at (list-at pose-coil-polys 0).pts 0).x, (list-at (list-at pose-flight-polys 0).pts 0).x, (list-at (list-at pose-land-polys 0).pts 0).x, (list-at (list-at pose-collapse-polys 0).pts 0).x]

  spot-want : List Real
  spot-want = [0.0092, 0.002, 0.6892, 0.5062, 0.6873, 0.6467, 0.6377]

  color-got : List Integer
  color-got = [(list-at pose-rest-polys 0).color, (list-at pose-collapse-polys 0).color]

  color-want : List Integer
  color-want = [9066271, 9066271]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cs-poly" poly-got poly-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cs-pts " pts-got pts-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cs-grad" grad-got grad-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cs-disp" disp-got disp-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "cs-spot" spot-got spot-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "cs-col " color-got color-want)
  end
